# Testing

## Two kinds of quality — do not confuse them

| | Software quality | Content quality |
|---|---|---|
| Question | Does the code do what we intended? | Is the generated content good? |
| Method | pytest / vitest | The quality gate + `evals/` harness |
| Determinism | Deterministic | Stochastic |
| Gate | Blocks merge | Blocks prompt activation |

**Never assert on model output in a unit test.** `assert "photosynthesis" in passage` will fail
randomly, be marked flaky, and take the useful tests around it down when someone deletes it. Model
behaviour is measured by the eval harness with rates and thresholds, never asserted by pytest with
equality.

## Pyramid

Bottom-heavy by design — `app/domain/` is pure specifically so it can be tested exhaustively and fast.

```
unit (500+, <10 s) > integration (~80, ~1 min) > contract (~30) > e2e (~10)
```

## Unit tests

- The rule engine gets the most effort. Every rule: passing case, failing case, edge case, `detail`
  assertion, and a property-based test asserting it never raises.
- Use `hypothesis` for invariants that must hold over all inputs.
- No I/O. No database. No model calls. If a unit test needs any of those, it is misplaced.

## Integration tests

- **Real Postgres 18 + pgvector via `testcontainers`. No mocked database.** The things most worth
  testing — constraints, triggers, transactions, vector search — are exactly what a mock would fake.
- Must-have cases: immutability trigger, approval-gate trigger, monotonic version numbers, tenant
  scoping enforcement, idempotency conflict, and **publication snapshots unaffected by later edits**.

That last one is the most valuable integration test in the suite: it verifies the core promise of the
data model and is exactly the guarantee that silently breaks during an unrelated refactor.

## E2E tests

- Run against `llama3.2:1b` — far too weak for real content, ideal for exercising the pipeline fast.
- **Test worker restart mid-run** and assert resumption from the last checkpoint. Durable execution
  that has never survived an actual restart is durable by assumption.

## Coverage

| Area | Target |
|------|--------|
| `domain/quality/` | 95 % |
| `domain/` | 90 % |
| `services/` | 80 % |
| `api/` | 75 % |
| `agents/` | 60 % |

`agents/` is deliberately low. Their real behaviour is model behaviour, measured properly by the eval
harness. Chasing coverage there produces tests that assert prompt strings — which break on every
prompt edit and verify nothing.

## Evals

- Statistical, not single-run: n ≥ 30 samples per task, compared with confidence intervals. One run
  showing a prompt is "better" is noise.
- Planted-defect tasks are the highest-value evaluation available — ground truth is exact because we
  planted it, and it needs no examiner.
- Prompt activation requires no regression beyond 5 points on the golden set.
- New `must` rules require ≤ 5 % false positives against authentic material.

## Never

- Never disable a database trigger to make a test pass. Write a test that satisfies it — that is what
  production will do.
- Never mock the rule engine. It is pure and fast; use the real thing.
- Never commit a test that depends on network access or a specific wall-clock time.
